4. Errors and fixes:
   - **Fork agent returned with 0 tool calls**: First attempt at researching opencode migration specifics via `subagent_type: "fork"` returned a suspiciously fast (7s), zero-tool-call result that just echoed context back. Fix: relaunched as a fresh `general-purpose` agent with an explicit instruction "You MUST use WebSearch and WebFetch tools — do not answer from prior knowledge alone, and do not return with zero tool calls" — this succeeded with real citations.
   - **Diagram text overlap** (first report): Fixed by adding background "halo" rects behind labels crossing diagram lines and adjusting font sizes/positions via manual coordinate calculation.
   - **"Fonts are tiny" complaint**: Root cause was NOT just font-size values but that `figure.diagram svg { width: 100%; }` was scaling the whole SVG (including nominal font sizes) down to fit container width. Initial fix (bumping font-size values ~20-25%) was insufficient because it didn't address the underlying scaling issue.
   - **CRITICAL: Diagram content silently clipped, invisible scrollbar**: User screenshot proved diagrams were losing content off-screen with a macOS-overlay scrollbar that never renders in static views. Root cause: `width: 100%` forces shrink-to-fit + `overflow-x: auto` hides overflow behind an undiscoverable scrollbar. On mobile, entire diagram halves were lost. Fix: (1) redesigned the widest diagrams (acquisition map, funnel-vs-fanout) as vertical stacks fitting within ~400-440px intrinsic width; (2) changed CSS from `width: 100%` to `max-width: 100%; height: auto;` (the standard responsive-image pattern) so diagrams render at full authored size when the container is wide enough and shrink-without-clipping when it isn't; (3) added explicit `width`/`height` HTML attributes to each SVG matching its viewBox. Verified via actual Playwright screenshots at both viewport sizes before republishing — this was the first time in the conversation a fix was empirically verified rather than just asserted.
   - **Duplicate "confirmed" text bug**: A callout title read "No — confirmed **CONFIRMED**" (word appeared once in the title text and once as the pill). Fixed by removing the redundant word from the title text, leaving just "No" + the pill.
   - **Decorative dots read as visual mistakes**: Both the hero CSS radial-gradient starfield background and hand-placed decorative `<circle>` stars inside the acquisition-map SVG were removed entirely, since sparse unstyled dots looked like rendering errors rather than intentional "space" texture.
   - **Column/callout-overuse critique** (user: "cramped", "used-car dealership with a million stickers"): User was correct. Fixed by (a) switching the dense 4-card grid to single column, (b) demoting 5 of 9 boxed callouts to plain text with no border/background, keeping only 4 genuine-warning callouts with a much lighter (border-only, no fill, no shadow) treatment.
   - **"Confidence: Medium" badge questioned**: Agreed it was fluff (aggregate confidence score with no actionable meaning, redundant with per-claim confirmed/likely/uncertain tags) and removed it from the status bar.
   - **Perceived topnav duplication bug (mobile screenshot)**: Investigated rigorously rather than assuming it was real. Confirmed via `shotcheck.mjs` (bounding box + computed styles on a direct viewport screenshot) and `scrolltest.mjs` (DOM query confirming exactly 1 `.topnav` element exists, plus a scroll-to-element + normal-viewport screenshot showing correct single rendering with no overlap) that the apparent mid-page duplicate topnav was a KNOWN Playwright `fullPage: true` screenshot-stitching artifact with `position: sticky` elements — not a real bug. This was NOT yet reported back to the user as a final conclusion at the point of summary (mid-investigation).
